WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:14.760
TIM COOK RETIRING:
On October 5, 2022, it officially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:21.180
became 11 years since Steve Jobs passed away. Many&nbsp;
believed that Apple would lose their touch without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:27.720
Steve and that things just wouldn’t be the same.&nbsp;
And while that may be true, there’s no arguing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:33.600
that Tim Cook has done a phenomenal job atleast&nbsp;
from a financial perspective. Tim has been able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:40.200
to grow Apple’s net income from $25 billion per&nbsp;
year which is already nuts to $100 billion per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:45.960
year. This has resulted in Apple stock growing&nbsp;
over 10X and Apple becoming the world’s first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:51.000
public trillion dollar company and eventually the&nbsp;
world’s first three trillion company. Considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.120
this tremendous performance, no shareholder is&nbsp;
really asking for Tim Cook to step down. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.280
at 61 years old, it looks like Tim himself is&nbsp;
ready to call it day within the next couple of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:07.800
years. While Tim has yet to reveal an exact date&nbsp;
for his retirement, he did give a couple of clues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:14.160
as to when he may be departing. In April of 2021,&nbsp;
Tim announced that he probably wouldn’t be running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:20.520
Apple in 10 years and that he’s looking to oversee&nbsp;
the launch of just 1 more product category. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.680
have speculated that this might be the Apple&nbsp;
car, but more than likely, he’s talking probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:31.860
talking about some sort of AR/VR headset. Rumors&nbsp;
surrounding this headset have become rampant and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.780
top analysts like Gurman and Kuo have suggested&nbsp;
that this headset may be launched as early as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:42.060
the end of this year. Even if that doesn’t&nbsp;
come to fruition, it seems like the headset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:47.460
should definitely come out within the next 1 to&nbsp;
2 years. I think it’s pretty safe to assume that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:52.020
Tim would probably want to stick around for a few&nbsp;
years after the launch to make sure everything is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:57.060
going smoothly before he retires. So, that would&nbsp;
put his estimated retirement date at somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:03.360
between 2025 and 2028. Combine this with the fact&nbsp;
that Tim’s next stock compensation package will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:09.060
finish paying out in 2025, and a retirement&nbsp;
shortly after that makes a lot of sense. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.740
this brings up the question, who will become&nbsp;
the next CEO of the world’s largest company.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:18.960
JEFF WILLIAMS:&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:23.700
Starting off the most likely choice, we have&nbsp;
the second most important person at Apple:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:30.000
Jeff Williams. Jeff is currently the COO or Chief&nbsp;
Operating Officer of Apple and he has quite an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.400
extensive background. In terms of education,&nbsp;
Jeff is actually an engineer having earned a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.020
mechanical engineering degree from North Carolina&nbsp;
State University. He followed this up with an MBA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:45.420
from Duke university. One of the first companies&nbsp;
that Jeff worked for after graduation was IBM.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:50.640
He joined the company in 1985 and spent the&nbsp;
next 13 years in a variety of roles related to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:56.340
engineering and operations. It wasn’t till 1998&nbsp;
that Jeff joined Apple as the head of Worldwide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:02.160
Procurement. From there, he was promoted to being&nbsp;
vice president of operations in 2004 and head of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:08.280
worldwide operations in 2010. And finally at the&nbsp;
end of 2015, he was promoted to his current role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:14.640
as COO. Operations isn’t the only thing he takes&nbsp;
care of though. After Jony Ive left Apple in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:20.160
Jeff oversaw Apple’s design department as well.&nbsp;
In terms of reasons why Jeff is a top candidate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.440
there’s a lot of reasons. First of all, he’s&nbsp;
an engineer, and in recent history we’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:30.240
seeing a trend away from business CEOs and&nbsp;
towards technical CEOs. Tim Cook himself is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.620
an engineer and the same thing could be said&nbsp;
about the CEOs of Microsoft, Google, Nvidia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:40.320
and AMD just to name a few. So, Jeff fits&nbsp;
that perfect modern CEO mold of having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.180
lot of business experience but also having a&nbsp;
lot of technical knowledge as well. We should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:51.420
also note that Tim Cook himself was COO before&nbsp;
moving up to CEO. Also, historically speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:59.160
76% of S&amp;P 500 CEOs were promoted from being COOs.&nbsp;
However, we have started to see a shift in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:06.240
trend. As of 2020, only 38% of CEOs were being&nbsp;
promoted from being COOs. Nearly the same amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:11.400
were being promoted from roles as divisional&nbsp;
CEOs or essentially vice presidents. Nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.440
if Tim Cook were to leave tomorrow unexpectedly,&nbsp;
Jeff Williams would almost definitely be the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:23.220
to takeover. However, Tim isn’t leaving tomorrow.&nbsp;
There’s still years before his departure, and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:28.620
alone reduces Jeff’s chances substantially.&nbsp;
Jeff is only two years younger than Tim at 59&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:35.280
years old. If Tim were to leave in 2028, Jeff&nbsp;
would already be 65 years old by then. And if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.020
Apple wants another leader who can lead for&nbsp;
more than 10 or 15 years, Jeff just doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:45.480
seem like the best choice. But, aside from this&nbsp;
one point, Jeff has everything going for him.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:49.680
EDDY CUE:&nbsp;

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:55.560
Moving onto our next candidate, we have Eddy Cue&nbsp;
who is the senior vice president of services. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:00.240
we’re talking about someone who’s loyal&nbsp;
to Apple, Eddy is definitely the guy as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.100
Apple has essentially been his entire career.&nbsp;
After graduating from Duke university with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:10.920
bachelors in computer science and economics, Eddy&nbsp;
joined Apple in 1989 at the age of 25, and he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:16.260
been there eversince. Eddy is one of those guys&nbsp;
who never worked on something that the average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:21.540
customer really ever acknowledges. He wasn’t the&nbsp;
one that designed the iPhone or the Air Pods or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:27.780
Face ID or anything like that. But, he has been&nbsp;
integral to the Apple experience since day one and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:34.320
he has hit homerun after homerun. First, in 1998,&nbsp;
he led development of the Apple website. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:41.100
in 2003, he led iTunes development, and in 2008,&nbsp;
he led App Store development. Since then, he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:46.920
also worked on Apple maps, iCloud, iMessage, Siri,&nbsp;
and literally every other Apple service you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:52.320
think of. Here’s a full list of all the services&nbsp;
that he’s overseeing right now. Basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:58.620
if it’s a service and it’s from Apple, this guy&nbsp;
has played some role in it. This means that Eddy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:04.140
is the mastermind behind the Apple experience. And&nbsp;
given that Apple is extremely focused on the Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:10.380
ecosystem and preventing users from leaving it,&nbsp;
Eddy could be the perfect choice for CEO. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:16.500
only so many iPhones that Apple can sell. And if&nbsp;
we’re not already at peak iPhone, we’ll definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:21.120
be there by the time Tim retires. So, Apple&nbsp;
can’t continue to increase profits and revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:26.340
just through hardware sales. To continue growing,&nbsp;
Apple needs to increase revenue and profit through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:31.200
services, and that’s exactly what they’ve been&nbsp;
trying to do eversince iPhone sales peaked a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.660
couple of years ago. But, despite that, Apple is&nbsp;
still very much a hardware company as opposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:41.880
a services company. Eddy, however, could change&nbsp;
that and get Apple to that $10 trillion mark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:47.880
From a business strategy point of view, I think&nbsp;
Eddy is by far the best choice. The next stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:52.920
for Apple is to transition into being a services&nbsp;
company and there’s simply no better guy to lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:59.640
that than Eddy. Like Jeff though, Eddy does fall&nbsp;
short in a few aspects. First of all, Eddy has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:04.260
virtually no experience outside of services. He&nbsp;
doesn’t have significant experience relating to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:10.200
operations, finance, branding, or any of the other&nbsp;
aspects that go into running a company. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:15.540
he’s not that much younger than Tim either coming&nbsp;
in at 57. Likely the biggest drawback, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:20.940
is that the Apple community as a whole isn’t the&nbsp;
biggest fan of Eddy. There’s literally reddit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:26.160
threads about why Apple should let go of Eddy.&nbsp;
The general consensus seems to be that Eddy used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:30.780
to lead great things, but that his current&nbsp;
work just doesn’t quite stack up. If we look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:36.000
past that though, Eddy is likely the best choice&nbsp;
in terms of making Apple into a services giant.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.137
GREG JOSWIAK:
Next up, we have yet another Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.137 --> 00:07:48.360
lifer Greg Joswiak. Greg is currently the senior&nbsp;
vice president of worldwide marketing. Greg is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:52.380
one of those people who ended up doing something&nbsp;
that’s completely unrelated from their educational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:58.920
background. Like Jeff and Eddy, Greg is also an&nbsp;
engineer at heart, but he’s never actually been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:03.900
an engineer in practice. He graduated from the&nbsp;
university of Michigan in 1986 with a computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:08.520
engineering degree and he was recruited to&nbsp;
Apple directly from college. Originally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:13.320
he was hired to work on the Macintosh but it&nbsp;
didn’t take him long to switch over to marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:18.900
and that’s what he’s doing for over 30 years now.&nbsp;
The first major marketing effort that he led at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:23.460
Apple was related to the Powerbook. After this,&nbsp;
he switched over to leading the marketing effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:28.860
of the iPod and eventually the iPhone. And just&nbsp;
two years ago, after Phil Schiller stepped down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:34.680
Greg took on the role of senior vice president.&nbsp;
Greg is kind of a tricky one to evaluate. He has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:38.820
a technical background, he clearly knows how to&nbsp;
market products, and he would likely lead Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:43.560
as product oriented company. But, while this&nbsp;
is the approach that has worked historically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:49.200
it’s not clear if this is the way moving forward.&nbsp;
As we previously touched on, it seems that most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:53.700
of Apple’s future growth lies within services&nbsp;
and this is not something that Greg really has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:59.100
experience in. To me, it seems like Greg simply&nbsp;
fits in his current role much better. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:04.080
isn’t to say that Greg would be a bad CEO. He’d&nbsp;
probably take Apple’s branding and marketing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:08.820
the next level and that could be helpful with new&nbsp;
product launches like the AR/VR headset and maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:14.340
even the Apple Car. But, it’s hard to see how&nbsp;
Greg would diversify Apple’s revenue and take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:19.980
them to the next level. And given that this is&nbsp;
usually shareholder’s top priority, Greg probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:26.940
isn’t the top choice. But given that Apple is a&nbsp;
product oriented company at their core and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:31.760
Greg is the most experienced Apple employee on the&nbsp;
executive team, we definitely can’t write him off.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:37.980
ODDBALLS:
Before we get into the fan favorite, I thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:42.000
we should cover some of the oddballs that have a&nbsp;
non negligible shot at the job. First we have Luca&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:47.940
Maestri who is the CFO of Apple. Luca has spent&nbsp;
his entire career within finance which makes sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:52.680
given that he has a bachelors in economics and a&nbsp;
masters in management. His experience includes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:58.920
GM, Nokia, Xerox, and of course Apple. If Apple&nbsp;
was experiencing a cash crunch or debt crisis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:03.960
maybe Luca would’ve been a good choice. But, at&nbsp;
this point, there doesn’t seem to be anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:09.360
going for Luca other than the fact that he’s also&nbsp;
C suite executive. Moving onto our next oddball,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:13.680
we have John Ternus who is the senior vice&nbsp;
president of hardware engineering. John has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:17.580
a bachelors in mechanical engineering from the&nbsp;
university of Pennsylvania and he’s been with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:21.840
Apple for a little more than 20 years now. He’s&nbsp;
currently in charge of leading the engineering for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:28.380
the iPhone, the iPad, the Mac, AirPods, and more.&nbsp;
John is definitely more of a product guy, but what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:33.360
makes him especially interesting is his age. John&nbsp;
is the youngest member of Apple’s executive team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:40.680
coming in at just 45 years old. So, he could&nbsp;
easily serve as CEO for 15 years or more. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:46.140
aside from that, nothing particularly sticks out&nbsp;
about John. Next up, we have the only woman on our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:51.540
list, Deirdre O’Brien. Deirdre is actually ranked&nbsp;
as one of the most powerful women in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:58.200
and given her position, that’s not too surprising.&nbsp;
Deirdre is in charge of the vast majority of Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:03.000
employees because she is the senior vice president&nbsp;
of retail and people. She has a degree in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:08.280
operations management and an MBA. And like many of&nbsp;
the other people on this list, she has spent her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:13.860
entire life at Apple. There’s no doubt that she’s&nbsp;
qualified for the position, but with so many other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:18.240
qualified individuals on this list, there doesn’t&nbsp;
seem to be any key reason why she would get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:24.840
job over any of them. And finally, to wrap up the&nbsp;
oddball list, we have Phil Schiller. Phil needs no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:30.240
introduction as he’s one of the most recognizable&nbsp;
Apple executives of all time. He’s been a part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:35.160
Apple’s keynotes for ages even during the Steve&nbsp;
Jobs era, and he was probably a top contender for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:42.420
CEO even in 2011. But, today he’s not that big of&nbsp;
a contender because he’s essentially retired. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:47.220
stepped down as a vice president a few years ago&nbsp;
and he holds an honorary title as Apple Fellow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:52.740
And at 62 years old, it’s unlikely that Apple&nbsp;
will choose someone who’s basically retired.

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:56.580
CRAIG FEDERIGHI:&nbsp;

00:11:56.580 --> 00:12:01.740
Someone else who doesn’t need an introduction is&nbsp;
Craig Fedeirghi who has become synonymous with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:08.460
Apple keynotes. Craig aka “hair force one” is by&nbsp;
far the most charismatic and lively presenter at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:13.800
Apple. And it seems that his charm has won&nbsp;
over much of the Apple fanbase. I suspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:18.960
that he reminds them of the Steve Jobs days.&nbsp;
In terms of qualifications, Craig is just as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:24.000
qualified as anyone else on this list. Craig has&nbsp;
a bachelors in computer science and electrical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:29.220
engineering from Berkeley. He also has a masters&nbsp;
in computer science also from Berkeley. Initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:34.620
he got acquainted with Steve Jobs at NeXT where&nbsp;
he led Enterprise Objects Framework. When Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:39.180
acquired NeXT, he became an Apple employee, but&nbsp;
he didn’t stick around there for long though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:45.600
In 1999, he would switch over to a company called&nbsp;
Ariba where he would eventually become CTO. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:49.980
Craig would eventually return to Apple in 2009&nbsp;
and has since become the senior vice president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:55.140
of software engineering. If we’re talking about&nbsp;
technical know how, I don’t think there’s anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.140 --> 00:13:00.480
on Apple’s executive team that has more techcnial&nbsp;
experience than Craig. Not to mention, Craig is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:05.340
relatively young for being an Apple executive.&nbsp;
While he’s not as young as John Ternus, he’s only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:11.820
53 years old meaning that he could easily serve&nbsp;
as CEO for 10 years. Maybe the only thing that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:16.500
not going in Craig’s favor is that he doesn’t&nbsp;
have much experience outside of software, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:22.620
this is true of many tech CEOs, so I don’t think&nbsp;
it’s much of a limiting factor. Now, Craig has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:28.500
stated in the past that he’s not looking to become&nbsp;
CEO, but honestly, that literally means nothing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:34.140
I mean what else is he supposed to say. Saying&nbsp;
that he wants to be CEO would just make him look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:40.260
power hungry and arrogant. So, ironically, saying&nbsp;
that he doesn’t want the job is actually his best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:45.540
play to get the job. Now, there is a subset of&nbsp;
the Apple community that strongly opposes Craig.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:50.760
They don’t think that the position of CEO should&nbsp;
go to someone that they view as an entertainer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:57.180
and a meme. But, you know, any CEO is going to&nbsp;
have haters, and it’s no different with Craig.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:01.020
APPLE’S NEXT CEO:&nbsp;

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:03.960
In the end, if I had to rank&nbsp;
the candidates by likelihood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:07.980
I would have to go with the traditional choices&nbsp;
which are of course Jeff Williams followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:12.540
up by Greg Joswiak. If Apple wanted to put a&nbsp;
strategic twist on this and focus on services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:19.440
they might go with Eddie Cue. While these guys are&nbsp;
no doubt the top choices today, given their ages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:22.860
I think there’s a good chance that Apple&nbsp;
decides to go with someone younger by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:29.040
time Tim retires. And if this is the case, I think&nbsp;
Craig has a strong shot at the job given his age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:33.840
charisma, and fandom. Personally, I think&nbsp;
he’d bring a refreshing spin to Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:39.180
and I’d love to see him get the job. But&nbsp;
that’s just what I think. Are you a fan of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:45.180
Craig being the next CEO of Apple? Comment that&nbsp;
down below. Also, drop a like if excited to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:49.800
the next phase of Apple. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:53.400
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

